WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:06.100
This is the indoor autonomy stack running a real warehouse floor.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.100
Indoors is where the silicon closes the loop, rather than advising.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.100
The vehicle steers, accelerates, and brakes itself.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.100
The truck is running an inbound put-away task.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:24.100
A 24-meter LiDAR at 5 Hz and a 57-degree camera fuse into the occupancy view on the right.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.100
The red ring traveling with the truck is the safety zone.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.100
It is not decoration. It gates the speed.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.100
And when a person enters it, the vehicle stops and speaks.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.100
You will hear that alert.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.100
The alert policy is configurable per site.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:44.100
Horn, voice, both, or silent, because that is an operations decision and not ours to make.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.100
Now the pick run.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:51.100
And then the peak hour floor, which is the scenario that decides whether a site can actually run this.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:56.100
Workers, peer vehicles, trolleys, and spills all have to be resolved.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.100
And recovery is explicit.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:03.100
The truck reroutes after a set stuck time rather than waiting for someone to notice.
